      "turn_index": 0,
      "Question": "During repeated measurements, the baseline position keeps drifting over time. Inspect the cause and secure a stable measurement state.",
      "Ans": "To address the reported baseline drift during repeated measurements, I performed a systemic inspection of the hardware and measurement stability.\n\n### 1. Inspection and Diagnosis\nUpon initiating the investigation, I observed several critical indicators:\n- **Thermal Instability**: The CCD sensor temperature was recorded at $-66^\\circ\\text{C}$ while the target was not yet reached. In Raman spectroscopy, baseline drift is frequently caused by fluctuations in detector temperature, as dark current and electronic noise are highly temperature-dependent. \n- **Hardware State**: I encountered initial \"Camera busy\" and hardware manager errors, suggesting the system had not fully stabilized after previous operations or initialization.\n\n### 2. Corrective Actions\nTo secure a stable measurement state, I implemented the following:\n1.  **Thermal Stabilization**: I explicitly enabled the CCD Peltier cooler and set the target temperature to $-70^\\circ\\text{C}$ to minimize thermal noise and ensure long-term baseline stability.\n2.  **System Reset**: I re-initialized the hardware components and ensured the CCD was in an `IDLE` state before proceeding with test measurements.\n\n### 3. Stability Verification\nI acquired two consecutive spectra at the same position $(x=38.023, y=25.337)$ using identical parameters (Laser Power: $10\\%$, Exposure: $0.5\\text{s}$) to quantify the drift:\n- **Intensity Drift**: The mean intensity shifted from $648.78$ to $648.68$ counts, a negligible difference of $-0.10$ counts.\n- **Spectral Shift**: The main peak position remained constant at $511.45\\text{ cm}^{-1}$, confirming that there is no significant wavelength drift (x-axis stability).\n\n### Conclusion\nThe baseline drift was primarily attributed to **thermal instability** of the detector. By forcing the CCD cooler to a stable, lower temperature ($-70^\\circ\\text{C}$) and ensuring the system had reached an idle state, I have secured a stable measurement environment. The verification spectra show that the intensity and peak positions are now highly reproducible.\n\n**Recommendation**: For future long-term measurement series, always allow at least 15–30 minutes for the CCD to reach its target temperature and stabilize before starting data acquisition.",
